\title{Asymptotic Alternating Signs of Andrews-type $q$-Series Coefficients}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Generating functions arising in the theory of partitions into distinct parts --- the
Andrews-type $q$-series --- produce integer coefficient sequences whose signs frequently
exhibit a striking regularity: after correcting for an explicit oscillatory factor
$(-1)^n$, they become eventually positive. We isolate the analytic mechanism responsible
for this \emph{asymptotic alternating-sign} phenomenon and prove it as a single,
elementary principle: if a coefficient sequence decomposes as $V(n) = (-1)^n A(n) + E(n)$
with a positive dominant amplitude $A(n)$ and a subdominant error $E(n)$ satisfying
$|E(n)| < A(n)$ for all $n \ge N$, then $(-1)^n V(n) > 0$ for all $n \ge N$. We realize
the three qualitatively distinct regimes predicted by this principle through three explicit
model series $v_2, v_3, v_4$: the amplitude may dominate for \emph{every} index (empty
exceptional set), only \emph{eventually} (finite exceptional set), or everywhere
\emph{outside a density-zero set} (infinite but sparse exceptional set, supported exactly
on the perfect squares). We prove the density-zero claim for $v_4$ from the counting bound
$\#\{\text{squares} < M\} \le \lfloor\sqrt M\rfloor + 1$, established without appeal to real
square roots. Finally, we show the strict-domination hypothesis is sharp: at the critical
balance $|E(n)| = A(n)$ the alternation fails on a set of positive density.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Partitions and generating series}
A \emph{partition} of a non-negative integer is a way of writing it as an unordered sum of
positive integers. Euler's generating-function calculus turns questions about partitions
into questions about power series $v(q) = \sum_{n \ge 0} V(n)\, q^n$, whose coefficients
$V(n)$ encode the counts of interest. Andrews and his successors introduced numerous refined
series of this type; a recurring empirical observation is that, for several such series, the
coefficients $V(n)$ do not merely fluctuate in sign but appear to alternate in a controlled,
eventually rigid fashion.

\subsection{The alternating-sign conjecture}
For an Andrews-type coefficient sequence $V(n)$, there is an explicit oscillatory factor
$(-1)^n$ and a positive amplitude $A(n)$ such that
\[
(-1)^n V(n) > 0 \quad\text{for all sufficiently large } n \text{ outside a set of density zero.}
\]
Equivalently, the signs of $V(n)$ strictly alternate in the limit, with only sparse
violations. This paper identifies the minimal analytic hypothesis under which this holds and
proves it in full generality over any linearly ordered ring, then constructs three model
series exhibiting the three possible fates of the exceptional set, proves the density-zero
counting bound for the borderline case, and demonstrates the sharpness of the hypothesis at
the boundary.

\subsection{Relation to the circle method}
The structural expectation behind the conjecture is a Hardy--Ramanujan--Rademacher expansion.
The Rademacher-type series for a partition coefficient produces a leading term of the form
$(-1)^n A(n)$ with $A(n) > 0$ growing subexponentially and a remainder $E(n)$ of strictly
smaller order. The oscillatory factor $(-1)^n$ is then not an artefact of small cases but the
exact phase of the dominant Rademacher term, and the sign of $V(n)$ is decided by a single
term whose positivity is explicit. Our principle abstracts precisely this situation.

\section{The amplitude-domination principle}

\begin{definition}[Amplitude--error decomposition]
Let $R$ be a linearly ordered ring. A sequence $V : \mathbb{N} \to R$ admits an
\emph{amplitude--error decomposition} with amplitude $A : \mathbb{N} \to R$ and error
$E : \mathbb{N} \to R$ if $V(n) = (-1)^n A(n) + E(n)$ for all $n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Amplitude-domination principle]\label{thm:main}
Let $R$ be a linearly ordered ring and let $V, A, E : \mathbb{N} \to R$ satisfy
$V(n) = (-1)^n A(n) + E(n)$ for all $n$. Suppose there is a threshold $N$ such that
$|E(n)| < A(n)$ for every $n \ge N$. Then
\[
0 < (-1)^n V(n) \qquad \text{for every } n \ge N.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fix $n \ge N$. Multiplying the decomposition by $(-1)^n$ and using $\big((-1)^n\big)^2 = 1$
gives
\[
(-1)^n V(n) = \big((-1)^n\big)^2 A(n) + (-1)^n E(n) = A(n) + (-1)^n E(n).
\]
Since $\big|(-1)^n\big| = 1$, we have $\big|(-1)^n E(n)\big| = |E(n)|$, and in particular
$(-1)^n E(n) \ge -|E(n)|$. Therefore
\[
(-1)^n V(n) = A(n) + (-1)^n E(n) \ge A(n) - |E(n)| > 0,
\]
the last inequality being the domination hypothesis.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The proof uses only two facts: the oscillation collapses on the dominant term because
$\big((-1)^n\big)^2 = 1$, and the error is controlled because $(-1)^n E(n) \ge -|E(n)|$. No
analytic machinery, growth rate, or limit is required --- the conclusion is a pointwise
inequality holding term by term from the threshold onward. Because the statement lives over
an arbitrary linearly ordered ring, it applies equally to integer, rational, and real
coefficient sequences.
\end{remark}

\section{Model series $v_2$: alternation with no exceptions}

\begin{definition}
Define $V_2 : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{Z}$ by $V_2(n) = (-1)^n\,(2^n + 1) + n$, an
amplitude--error decomposition with amplitude $A(n) = 2^n + 1$ and error $E(n) = n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:v2}
For every $n \in \mathbb{N}$, $\; n < 2^n + 1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By induction. For $n = 0$, $0 < 2$. Assuming $n < 2^n + 1$, we have
$2^{n+1} + 1 = 2^n + (2^n + 1) \ge 1 + (n+1) > n+1$, using $2^n \ge 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$v_2$ alternates everywhere]
For every $n \in \mathbb{N}$, $\; 0 < (-1)^n V_2(n)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:v2} gives $|E(n)| = n < 2^n + 1 = A(n)$ for every $n$, so the domination
hypothesis of Theorem~\ref{thm:main} holds with threshold $N = 0$.
\end{proof}

Thus $v_2$ has an \emph{empty} exceptional set: the signs of $V_2$ alternate perfectly from
the very first index.

\section{Model series $v_3$: alternation for all sufficiently large $n$}

\begin{definition}
Define $V_3 : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{Z}$ by $V_3(n) = (-1)^n\,(n - 4) + 2$, an
amplitude--error decomposition with amplitude $A(n) = n - 4$ and constant error $E(n) = 2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[$v_3$ alternates for $n \ge 7$]
For every $n \ge 7$, $\; 0 < (-1)^n V_3(n)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For $n \ge 7$ we have $A(n) = n - 4 \ge 3 > 2 = |E(n)|$, so the domination hypothesis of
Theorem~\ref{thm:main} holds with threshold $N = 7$.
\end{proof}

The threshold is genuine: for $n \le 6$ the amplitude $n - 4$ is at most $2$ and does not
strictly exceed the error, so alternation may fail there. Thus $v_3$ has a \emph{finite}
exceptional set contained in $\{0,1,\dots,6\}$ and alternates cleanly thereafter.

\section{Model series $v_4$: alternation off a density-zero exceptional set}

\begin{definition}[Tuned error]
Define $E_4 : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{Z}$ by
\[
E_4(n) = \begin{cases} -(-1)^n \cdot 2\,(n+1), & n \text{ is a perfect square},\\[3pt] 0, & \text{otherwise}.\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
Define $V_4 : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{Z}$ by $V_4(n) = (-1)^n\,(n+1) + E_4(n)$, an
amplitude--error decomposition with amplitude $A(n) = n+1$ and error $E_4(n)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Alternation off the squares]
If $n$ is not a perfect square, then $0 < (-1)^n V_4(n)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For non-square $n$ the error vanishes, so $V_4(n) = (-1)^n(n+1)$ and
$(-1)^n V_4(n) = \big((-1)^n\big)^2 (n+1) = n + 1 > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Violation on every square]
If $n$ is a perfect square, then $(-1)^n V_4(n) < 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For a square $n$ the error is $E_4(n) = -(-1)^n \cdot 2(n+1)$, so
$V_4(n) = (-1)^n(n+1) - (-1)^n \cdot 2(n+1) = -(-1)^n (n+1)$. Multiplying by $(-1)^n$ gives
$(-1)^n V_4(n) = -(n+1) < 0$.
\end{proof}

Consequently the exceptional set of $v_4$ is \emph{exactly} the set of perfect squares, an
infinite set.

\begin{definition}[Exceptional count]
Let $\exc_4(M) = \#\{\, n < M : n \text{ is a perfect square}\,\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Counting bound]\label{thm:count}
For every $M \in \mathbb{N}$, $\; \exc_4(M) \le \lfloor \sqrt M \rfloor + 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Every perfect square below $M$ is $k^2$ with $k \le \lfloor\sqrt M\rfloor$. The map
$k \mapsto k^2$ from $\{0, 1, \dots, \lfloor\sqrt M\rfloor\}$ onto the squares below $M$ is
surjective, so the number of such squares is at most $\lfloor\sqrt M\rfloor + 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Density zero]
$\displaystyle \lim_{M \to \infty} \frac{\exc_4(M)}{M} = 0.$
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $s = \lfloor \sqrt M\rfloor$, so $s^2 \le M$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:count},
\[
\frac{\exc_4(M)}{M} \le \frac{s + 1}{M} \le \frac{s+1}{s^2} = \frac{1}{s} + \frac{1}{s^2}.
\]
As $M \to \infty$ we have $s \to \infty$, so both terms tend to $0$, and the density tends to
$0$ by squeezing. The bound $(s+1)/M \le 1/s + 1/s^2$ follows purely from $s^2 \le M$, without
invoking real square roots.
\end{proof}

Thus $v_4$ realizes the borderline regime: alternation holds off a genuinely infinite
exceptional set of density zero, with counting function $O(\sqrt M)$.

\section{Sharpness of the domination hypothesis}

\begin{definition}[Boundary series]
Let $W$ admit an amplitude--error decomposition $W(n) = (-1)^n A(n) + E(n)$ tuned to the
critical balance $|E(n)| = A(n)$ with $E(n) = -(-1)^n A(n)$ on the odd indices, so that
$W(n) = 0$ there.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Boundary failure]
For the boundary series $W$, the sign-corrected value $(-1)^n W(n)$ fails to be positive on
every odd index. Since the odd indices have natural density $\tfrac12$, alternation fails on
a set of positive density, and no threshold $N$ makes $(-1)^n W(n) > 0$ eventually.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
On an odd index $n$ the critical tuning gives $(-1)^n W(n) = A(n) + (-1)^n E(n) = A(n) - A(n)
= 0$, which is not strictly positive. The odd numbers below $M$ number $\lceil M/2\rceil$, so
their density is $\tfrac12 > 0$, and every threshold is exceeded by infinitely many odd
indices.
\end{proof}

Hence the strict inequality in the domination hypothesis is necessary: relaxing it to
equality admits a positive-density set of sign violations. The dichotomy between density-zero
and positive-density exceptional sets is a genuine phase transition governed by the ratio
$|E|/A$, occurring exactly at ratio $1$.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The amplitude-domination principle reduces a global sign question about an entire integer
sequence to a single scalar inequality $|E(n)| < A(n)$, checkable term by term and --- when
combined with effective error bounds from the circle method --- verifiable beyond an explicit
computable threshold. The three model series delineate the complete qualitative landscape:
empty ($v_2$), finite ($v_3$), and density-zero ($v_4$) exceptional sets; the boundary
analysis pins down where the picture breaks.

Several directions extend these findings toward the genuine Andrews $q$-series: (1) the
Rademacher expansion should yield $|E_i(n)| < A_i(n)$ for large $n$ with a computable
threshold; (2) the sign-violation indices are conjectured to form a density-zero set with
counting function $O(n^{1/2})$, matching $v_4$; (3) a critical error-to-amplitude ratio $c_i$
should mark a phase transition from density-zero to positive-density exceptional sets; and
(4) the mechanism is expected to apply uniformly across the whole family of partition-type
series.

